data: {"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"input_json_delta","partial_json":"{\"kind\": \"decision\", \"title\": \"Store carts in Postgres or Redis?\", \"body\": \"Carts currently live in a 4 KB session cookie, so long carts get truncated. Moving them server-side means the cookie only holds a cart ID.\\n\\nExample: a shopper adds 40 items, and the cookie silently drops the last 15.\\n\\n**Redis:** Reads and writes are very fast, and per-key TTLs suit abandoned carts. The downsides are a new piece of infrastructure to run if you don't already have it, memory cost, and data loss on eviction or failover unless persistence is configured.\\n\\n**Postgres:** Carts survive restarts and are queryable for abandoned-cart emails, analytics, and joins with products and inventory. Latency is slightly higher, which is fine at cart sizes. You need a cleanup job to delete stale carts.\\n\\n**My pick:** Postgres, since carts are business data and you likely already run it. Choose Redis only if you already operate it and the carts are disposable.\", \"actions\": [{\"label\":\"Use Postgres (Recommended)\",\"kind\":\"resolve\",\"recommended\":true},{\"label\":\"Use Redis\",\"kind\":\"resolve\"}]}"}}

